1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:02,766
You think it is your boss who makes you afraid to switch jobs

2
00:00:03,066 --> 00:00:06,966
But often what really slows you down is that one fixed payment every month

3
00:00:07,133 --> 00:00:08,233
Especially rent

4
00:00:08,366 --> 00:00:09,933
You want to turn down a last-minute request

5
00:00:09,933 --> 00:00:11,300
But then you think about next week's payment

6
00:00:11,366 --> 00:00:13,000
And the words die on your lips

7
00:00:13,033 --> 00:00:14,600
You want to try a new direction

8
00:00:14,633 --> 00:00:16,533
But then you think about the deposit utilities and moving costs

9
00:00:16,533 --> 00:00:18,066
And you figure waiting a bit longer is fine

10
00:00:18,233 --> 00:00:20,466
So this video makes just one point

11
00:00:20,633 --> 00:00:22,200
A person's real range of choices

12
00:00:22,233 --> 00:00:24,000
does not just depend on how much they earn

13
00:00:24,133 --> 00:00:26,366
It also depends on how much they must spend every month

14
00:00:26,366 --> 00:00:28,133
Picture an ordinary evening

15
00:00:28,133 --> 00:00:29,266
You are about to shut down your computer

16
00:00:29,266 --> 00:00:30,766
Your phone lights up with a message

17
00:00:30,866 --> 00:00:32,900
Can you take another look at this version tonight

18
00:00:32,966 --> 00:00:34,000
You are already exhausted

19
00:00:34,033 --> 00:00:35,633
And you know this isn't urgent

20
00:00:35,700 --> 00:00:37,000
But you still reply

21
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:37,366
Sure

22
00:00:37,366 --> 00:00:38,100
I'll take a look

23
00:00:38,100 --> 00:00:39,766
It's not necessarily that you have no backbone

24
00:00:39,833 --> 00:00:41,533
Nor that you are especially easygoing

25
00:00:41,533 --> 00:00:42,766
You just know all too well

26
00:00:42,833 --> 00:00:44,000
Rent comes out next week

27
00:00:44,033 --> 00:00:45,400
The credit card is due this month

28
00:00:45,533 --> 00:00:46,966
And the fridge needs restocking

29
00:00:47,266 --> 00:00:48,766
Moving isn't something you can just do on a whim

30
00:00:48,766 --> 00:00:50,033
That's when you realize

31
00:00:50,133 --> 00:00:52,066
Rent is not just a simple price

32
00:00:52,166 --> 00:00:54,466
It's more like the minimum cost just to keep your life running

33
00:00:54,666 --> 00:00:55,966
As soon as the month begins

34
00:00:55,966 --> 00:00:57,800
That money is already waiting for you up front

35
00:00:57,833 --> 00:00:59,000
So for many people the pressure

36
00:00:59,033 --> 00:01:00,166
isn't just having too much work

37
00:01:00,266 --> 00:01:02,266
It's knowing they can't easily stop

38
00:01:02,533 --> 00:01:03,633
You can rest when you're tired

39
00:01:03,666 --> 00:01:05,733
But not being able to stop makes people more and more cautious

40
00:01:05,733 --> 00:01:07,566
You clearly want to say I'll pass this time

41
00:01:07,566 --> 00:01:08,666
But in the end it becomes

42
00:01:08,700 --> 00:01:09,566
Let me push through a little longer

43
00:01:09,566 --> 00:01:10,800
Many people look at rent

44
00:01:10,833 --> 00:01:12,900
and only see three or five or eight thousand a month

45
00:01:12,966 --> 00:01:14,500
As if it just means living a bit more expensively

46
00:01:14,566 --> 00:01:16,266
But what rent really affects

47
00:01:16,266 --> 00:01:17,400
is your ability to take risks

48
00:01:17,433 --> 00:01:19,033
People with lower fixed costs

49
00:01:19,066 --> 00:01:20,400
can hesitate a few more days

50
00:01:20,466 --> 00:01:21,833
can take a break for a while

51
00:01:21,933 --> 00:01:24,466
can take a job whose early income is less stable

52
00:01:24,566 --> 00:01:26,466
can spend a few months learning a new skill

53
00:01:26,466 --> 00:01:27,366
Because they know

54
00:01:27,366 --> 00:01:29,366
their life doesn't need to run at full speed right away

55
00:01:29,366 --> 00:01:30,833
But people with high fixed costs

56
00:01:30,900 --> 00:01:32,666
do the math before every choice

57
00:01:32,666 --> 00:01:33,866
Can I get through this month

58
00:01:33,900 --> 00:01:35,300
Is there a cushion next month

59
00:01:35,433 --> 00:01:36,500
If I switch direction

60
00:01:36,566 --> 00:01:38,166
who pays for the gap in between

61
00:01:38,166 --> 00:01:38,833
You see

62
00:01:38,833 --> 00:01:40,000
This isn't a question of courage

63
00:01:40,133 --> 00:01:41,200
It's a question of the ledger

64
00:01:41,233 --> 00:01:43,600
The higher a person's mandatory monthly spending

65
00:01:43,733 --> 00:01:45,533
the less uncertainty they can bear

66
00:01:45,533 --> 00:01:47,100
This sounds a little cold

67
00:01:47,100 --> 00:01:48,200
but it's very useful

68
00:01:48,300 --> 00:01:49,900
Because it takes a lot of emotional problems

69
00:01:49,900 --> 00:01:51,566
and re-translates them into structural problems

70
00:01:51,566 --> 00:01:52,733
It's not that you have no ideas

71
00:01:52,733 --> 00:01:55,266
It's that the cost of living has squeezed your room to experiment

72
00:01:55,266 --> 00:01:57,166
So when judging whether a place is worth it

73
00:01:57,233 --> 00:01:58,766
don't only ask whether you can afford the rent

74
00:01:58,866 --> 00:02:00,000
Ask three more questions

75
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:00,666
First

76
00:02:00,666 --> 00:02:02,666
Does it actually save you time

77
00:02:02,866 --> 00:02:03,800
If it costs a bit more

78
00:02:03,833 --> 00:02:05,833
but cuts an hour off your commute every day

79
00:02:05,933 --> 00:02:07,000
lets you sleep more soundly

80
00:02:07,033 --> 00:02:08,700
gives you energy for your own things

81
00:02:08,733 --> 00:02:09,933
then it may well be worth it

82
00:02:09,933 --> 00:02:10,566
Second

83
00:02:10,566 --> 00:02:12,633
Does it only help you keep up appearances

84
00:02:12,733 --> 00:02:14,200
Looks nicer when friends come over

85
00:02:14,233 --> 00:02:15,833
The address sounds a bit more comfortable

86
00:02:15,866 --> 00:02:17,200
More amenities downstairs

87
00:02:17,233 --> 00:02:18,733
These have value of course

88
00:02:18,733 --> 00:02:20,533
but you need to know what you are buying

89
00:02:20,533 --> 00:02:21,533
If this appearance

90
00:02:21,533 --> 00:02:23,800
forces you to count every penny month after month

91
00:02:23,866 --> 00:02:24,966
it may not be worth it

92
00:02:24,966 --> 00:02:25,666
Third

93
00:02:25,666 --> 00:02:27,566
Does it leave you room to adjust

94
00:02:27,566 --> 00:02:28,700
A truly good place to live

95
00:02:28,700 --> 00:02:29,866
isn't the more expensive the better

96
00:02:29,966 --> 00:02:31,166
nor the cheaper the better

97
00:02:31,166 --> 00:02:32,666
It's one that lets you live

98
00:02:32,666 --> 00:02:33,600
and still turn around

99
00:02:33,633 --> 00:02:35,100
When you look at the bill at month's end

100
00:02:35,166 --> 00:02:36,200
you're not left with just one thought

101
00:02:36,233 --> 00:02:37,466
Got to hang on till next month again

102
00:02:37,466 --> 00:02:38,433
but can also think

103
00:02:38,466 --> 00:02:39,866
I still have a little room to work with

104
00:02:39,866 --> 00:02:41,066
So what should an ordinary person do

105
00:02:41,066 --> 00:02:42,700
I suggest starting with three small things

106
00:02:42,700 --> 00:02:43,300
First

107
00:02:43,300 --> 00:02:44,533
Work out your rent ratio

108
00:02:44,533 --> 00:02:47,800
Add up fixed costs like rent utilities commute and takeout

109
00:02:47,833 --> 00:02:49,400
and see what share of your income they take

110
00:02:49,433 --> 00:02:50,966
Don't just count the rent itself

111
00:02:51,066 --> 00:02:53,600
Count the total cost that comes with this place

112
00:02:53,600 --> 00:02:54,266
Second

113
00:02:54,266 --> 00:02:55,600
Keep a three-month cushion

114
00:02:55,633 --> 00:02:57,600
These three months aren't for doing nothing

115
00:02:57,666 --> 00:02:59,766
They're so you don't panic when things change

116
00:02:59,966 --> 00:03:01,366
If your work pace suddenly shifts

117
00:03:01,366 --> 00:03:02,500
you have time to look at the road ahead

118
00:03:02,500 --> 00:03:03,766
If your health isn't great

119
00:03:03,766 --> 00:03:04,766
you can slow down

120
00:03:04,766 --> 00:03:06,066
If you want to try a new direction

121
00:03:06,066 --> 00:03:08,500
you won't be chased by the bills right from the start

122
00:03:08,500 --> 00:03:09,133
Third

123
00:03:09,133 --> 00:03:10,866
Calculate your home and your career together

124
00:03:10,866 --> 00:03:12,900
Some jobs look like they pay more

125
00:03:12,966 --> 00:03:14,600
but they require you to live somewhere pricier

126
00:03:14,633 --> 00:03:15,600
commute longer

127
00:03:15,633 --> 00:03:16,966
rely more on takeout

128
00:03:16,966 --> 00:03:18,100
and rest less

129
00:03:18,100 --> 00:03:19,266
After totaling it all up

130
00:03:19,300 --> 00:03:21,000
you may find your income went up

131
00:03:21,033 --> 00:03:22,366
but your breathing room didn't

132
00:03:22,366 --> 00:03:24,333
This is what rent reminds ordinary people

133
00:03:24,333 --> 00:03:25,933
A home isn't better the cheaper it is

134
00:03:25,933 --> 00:03:27,466
and life isn't better the more you scrimp

135
00:03:27,466 --> 00:03:30,166
What matters is whether after those fixed costs come out each month

136
00:03:30,233 --> 00:03:32,400
you still have a little room to arrange your own life

137
00:03:32,433 --> 00:03:35,333
Many people think freedom only comes after earning more money

138
00:03:35,333 --> 00:03:36,166
But sometimes

139
00:03:36,300 --> 00:03:38,600
freedom starts with a little less fixed cost

140
00:03:38,633 --> 00:03:40,000
Next time we'll talk about the rice bowl

141
00:03:40,033 --> 00:03:41,666
Why the Chinese call a stable job

142
00:03:41,666 --> 00:03:42,600
not a job

143
00:03:42,633 --> 00:03:43,566
but a rice bowl
